**Blockchain basics**

A blockchain is a distributed, tamper‑proof ledger that records transactions across many computers (nodes). The key ideas that make it work are:

| Concept | What it does |
|---------|---------------|
| **Block** | A container that holds a group of valid transactions plus a small amount of metadata (e.g., timestamp, block version). |
| **Cryptographic hash** | A fixed‑size “digital fingerprint” produced by a one‑way function (e.g., SHA‑256). Changing any data in the block changes its hash completely. |
| **Previous block hash** | Each block includes the hash of the block that came before it, creating a chain. This links blocks together and makes tampering evident. |
| **Merkle tree** | Transactions are organized in a binary tree; the root hash (Merkle root) summarizes all the txns in the block. |
| **Consensus algorithm** | A set of rules that network participants follow to agree on which block to add next (e.g., Proof‑of‑Work, Proof‑of‑Stake). |
| **Distributed network** | Copies of the ledger (the full blockchain) are kept by many nodes worldwide, so there’s no single point of failure. |
| **Immutability** | Because altering a past block would change its hash, breaking the “previous hash” link, the network would reject the altered block unless an attacker controls the majority of the network (≥ 51 %). |

---

