A **blockchain** is a distributed, append‑only ledger that records transactions (or other data) across many computers (called **nodes**) in a way that makes the record extremely hard to alter retroactively. The key ideas are:

| Concept | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| **Block** | A container that holds a batch of transactions (or other data) plus a **header** containing metadata. |
| **Cryptographic hash** | A fixed‑size “digital fingerprint” produced by a one‑way function (e.g., SHA‑256). Any change in the block’s contents changes its hash dramatically. |
| **Previous block hash** | Each block header contains the hash of the preceding block, creating a chain. This links blocks together and makes tampering evident. |
| **Consensus mechanism** | A set of rules that lets the network agree on which block to add next (e.g., Proof‑of‑Work, Proof‑of‑Stake). |
| **Decentralization** | Full copies of the ledger are kept by many nodes, so there is no single point of failure. |
| **Immutability** | Because altering a block changes its hash, and every subsequent block would need to be re‑computed, the cost of rewriting history is astronomically high. |

---

