2026/02/26 19:13:38 [TASK] AI inference result: ## A High‑Level Overview of How a Blockchain Works  

### 1. The Core Idea  
A blockchain is a **distributed digital ledger** that records transactions (or any piece of data) across many computers (called *nodes*) spread around the world.  
- **Immutable** – once a record is written, it cannot be altered without the consensus of the network.  
- **Transparent** – anyone can (in most public chains) view the ledger, though they may not see personal identities.  
- **Decentralized** – there is no single central authority; the ledger is maintained collectively.

### 2. What a “Block” Looks Like  
Each block contains three main things:

| Component | What It Holds |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Header** | • **Version** – protocol version<br>• **Previous Block Hash** – a fingerprint of the block that came before (links the blocks together)<br>• **Merkle Root** – a hash that summarizes all the transactions in the block<br>• **Timestamp** – when the block was created<br>• **Nonce** – a random number used in the proof‑of‑work process |
| **Transaction List** | The actual data – e.g., “Alice sends Bob 1 BTC”, a smart‑contract state change, a supply‑chain entry, etc. |
| **Block Hash** | A cryptographic hash of the entire header (computed after the block is assembled). Changing any tiny piece of data changes this hash dramatically. |

### 3. Linking Blocks: The Chain  
- The **previous block hash** in a new block points to the hash of the block that was created just before it.  
- This creates a **chain**: each block references the preceding one. If someone tries to tamper with an old block, its hash changes, breaking the link to the next block, which then breaks the link to the next, and so on. The network detects the inconsistency and rejects the tampered version.

### 4. Consensus: How the Network Agrees on the “True” Ledger  
Because many nodes hold a copy of the ledger, they must constantly agree on which blocks are legitimate. Different **consensus mechanisms** achieve this:

| Mechanism | How It Works (Simplified) | Typical Use |
|-----------|---------------------------|--------------|
| **Proof‑of‑Work (PoW)** | Nodes (miners) compete to solve a hard mathematical puzzle. The first to solve it gets to add the next block. The rest of the network verifies the solution. | Bitcoin, early Ethereum |
| **Proof‑of‑Stake (PoS)** | Validators lock up (stake) some of their cryptocurrency as collateral. The protocol randomly picks a validator to propose the next block; others verify it. If they act dishonestly, they lose their stake. | Ethereum (after The Merge), Cardano, Solana (some hybrid) |
| **Byzantine Fault Tolerant (BFT)** (e.g., Practical BFT) | A set of known validators vote on the next block; the protocol tolerates up to ~⅓ malicious or failed nodes. | Private/permissioned chains (Hyperledger Fabric) |
| **Delegated Proof‑of‑Stake (DPoS)** | Token holders vote for a small number of delegates who take turns proposing blocks. | EOS, TRON |

**Why consensus matters:** It makes it virtually impossible for an attacker to rewrite history, because they would need to control >50% (or >⅔, depending on the algorithm) of the network’s computing
