## Core Structure

**Blocks** contain:
- Transaction data (who sent what to whom)
- A timestamp
- A reference to the previous block (called a *hash*)
- Its own unique hash

These blocks link together chronologically, forming a **chain**. Changing any block would change its hash, breaking the chain and making tampering immediately obvious.

## Key Mechanisms

**Distributed network** — Thousands of computers (nodes) each maintain a complete copy of the ledger. No single entity controls it.

**Consensus** — Before a new block is added, nodes must agree it's valid. Common methods include:
- *Proof of Work* (computational puzzles, used by Bitcoin)
- *Proof of Stake* (economic stake, used by Ethereum post-merge)

**Cryptography** — Public and private keys secure ownership and authorize transactions without revealing identities.

## Why It Matters

| Feature | Result |
|--------|--------|
| Decentralization | No single point of failure or control |
| Transparency | All participants see the same history |
| Immutability | Altering past records requires impossible coordination |

## Limitations

- Energy intensive (especially Proof of Work)
- Slower than traditional databases
- Scalability challenges
- "Garbage in, garbage out" — accuracy depends on initial data entry

