---

## 1. Core Components  

| Component | What it is | Why it matters |
|-----------|------------|----------------|
| **Block** | A container that holds a set of verified transactions and a small amount of metadata. | Provides the atomic unit of data that gets appended to the chain. |
| **Cryptographic hash** | A fixed‑size “digital fingerprint” produced by a hash function (e.g., SHA‑256). | Guarantees data integrity – any change in a block’s contents changes its hash completely. |
| **Previous block hash** | The hash of the block that came immediately before the current one. | Links blocks together, forming a chain; altering any past block would break the chain. |
| **Merkle tree** | A binary tree of transaction hashes that collapses all transaction data into a single “Merkle root.” | Enables efficient, secure verification of a transaction’s presence in a block. |
| **Consensus algorithm** | The protocol nodes use to agree on which block to add next (e.g., Proof‑of‑Work, Proof‑of‑Stake). | Prevents double‑spending and ensures all copies of the ledger are identical. |
| **Node** | A computer that keeps a copy of the entire blockchain (full node) or a subset (light node). | Provides decentralization and redundancy. |
| **Transaction** | A signed message that transfers value or executes a piece of code (in smart‑contract platforms). | The basic operation recorded on the ledger. |

---

